  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThrough Tonight: Local radars still show light showers across the \nsouthern half of the CWA early this morning. These showers were \noccuring north of a surface front but being maintained by a southern \nstream shortwave trough across the region. This shortwave will \ncontinue shifting east through tonight. The large >1024mb surface \nhigh centered over the mid Mississippi valley early this morning \nwill shift east as well but help push south a drier airmass with \nsurface dew points in the 50s-60s. Lower 60 dew points were already \nbeing observed over our northwest zones with dew points in the 50s \njust north of our CWA. By Tuesday morning dew points in the 50s will \nbe nearly along and northwest of the Natchez Trace. As the shortwave \ntrough shifts east and the drier air surges south today, rain \nchances will gradually taper off from the north. By this evening \nonly very low chances of light rain will be over our southeast most \nzones but this chance for rain will end by mid evening. Despite the \ncold front and drier air, temperatures will be cooler but remain \nabove normal through Tuesday morning. /22/ \n\nTuesday-Saturday:\n\nNorthwest flow aloft will continue to re-enforce the dry air over \nthe region through mid-week. Above-average temperatures (highs: \nupper 80s/lows:upper 50s/60s) will remain through majority of the \nweek. Model consensus continue to indicate an organized low pressure \nand potential tropical development late through the week into next \nweekend. The National Hurricane Center has added a 50 percent chance \nof development in this region next week. Will continue to monitor \nfor possible future development. Regardless of development, tropical \nmoisture may push into the region, increasing rain chances (25-50%) \nover the weekend. Temperature will return to near-average \ntemperatures (highs: ranging in the 80s/lows: ranging in the 60s) \nthrough the end of the weekend. /SW/",
